# 🔒 CLOUDFLARE PAGES APPENDS, IT DOES NOT OVERRIDE, AND ASSUMING OTHERWISE SHIPPED A
# CONTRADICTORY HEADER (2026-09-03). This file first carried `/assets/*` at one hour followed by
# specific rules at a year, on the belief that the later rule wins for a header both set. It does
# not. Measured on the live domain minutes after deploying:
#   cache-control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate, public, max-age=31536000, immutable
# Two max-age directives and must-revalidate beside immutable, in one header. A parser takes the
# first max-age, so the effect was the old hour: no regression, and none of the improvement.
# 🔒 SO NO TWO RULES HERE MAY MATCH THE SAME PATH FOR THE SAME HEADER. The patterns below are
# disjoint by construction and tools/headers-gate.py proves it, because the failure is invisible
# in this file and only appears in a response header nobody reads.

# A year, immutable, for the files whose URL carries their own content hash. stamp-assets.py
# rewrites every reference to `?v=<sha256[:10]>`, so a changed file is a changed URL and a stale
# pairing is impossible rather than unlikely. That is exactly the precondition immutable needs,
# and until now the site paid a revalidation round trip for the stylesheet and the script on
# every navigation while already holding the guarantee that made it unnecessary.
# 🔒 THE PRECONDITION IS ENFORCED, NOT ASSUMED: check-site.py fails a page that references an
# asset with no ?v=, which under these headers would be a file cached for a year at one URL.
/assets/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/assets/shots/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/assets/site.min.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/assets/site.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/assets/site.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# And an hour, revalidated, for the ones whose URL never changes. /assets/lang-check is the one
# that matters: it is the diagnostic a person is sent to when the language redirect is behaving
# oddly, and a year-long cache would hand them last month's answer to this month's question.
# Both spellings, because Pages 308s the .html form to the extensionless one.
/assets/lang-check.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

/assets/lang-check
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

/assets/og.jpg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

/assets/og-es.jpg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

/assets/icon-512.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

/assets/appicon-1024.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate
